(a) Supplements to this Code shall be prepared and printed whenever authorized or directed by the city. A supplement to the Code shall include all substantive permanent and general parts of ordinances adopted during the period covered by the supplement and all changes made thereby in the Code. The pages of a supplement shall be so numbered that they will fit properly into the Code and will, where necessary, replace pages which have become obsolete or partially obsolete, and the new pages shall be so prepared that, when they have been inserted, the Code will be current through the date of the adoption of the latest ordinance included in the supplement.
(b) In preparing a supplement to this Code, all portions of the Code which have been repealed shall be excluded from the Code by the omission thereof from reprinted pages.
(c) When preparing a supplement to this Code, the person authorized to prepare the supplement may make formal, nonsubstantive changes in ordinances and parts of ordinances included in the supplement, insofar as it is necessary to do so to embody them into a unified Code. For example, the person may:
(1) Organize the ordinance material into appropriate subdivisions;
(2) Provide appropriate catchlines, headings and titles for sections and other subdivisions of the Code printed in the supplement and make changes in such catchlines, headings and titles;
(3) Assign appropriate numbers to sections and other subdivisions to be inserted in the Code and, where necessary to accommodate new material, change existing section or other subdivision numbers;
(4) Change the words "this ordinance" or words of the same meaning to "this chapter," "this article," "this division," etc., as the case may be, or to "sections _____ to _____" (inserting section numbers to indicate the sections of the Code which embody the substantive sections of the ordinance incorporated into the Code); and
(5) Make other nonsubstantive changes necessary to preserve the original meaning of ordinance sections inserted into the Code; but in no case shall the person make any change in the meaning or effect of ordinance material included in the supplement or already embodied in the Code.

155:
(a) Unless otherwise permitted by law or this chapter, no license which has been applied for shall be denied, and no license which has been issued shall be suspended or revoked, except for any ground, cause, good cause or due cause as hereinafter defined.
(b) The terms "ground", "cause", "good cause" or "due cause" (hereafter these terms may be collectively referred to as "ground") for the purposes of this chapter shall include, but not be limited to:
(1) Violation of any provision of this chapter;
(2) Conviction of any felony by the licensee or any employee of licensee or any person holding an interest in the license;
(3) Knowing falsification of any fact given in an application for a license or bearing upon the applicant's or licensee's qualification for a license;
(4) Failure or refusal to meet or maintain any requirement or standard prescribed by this chapter as a condition to or qualification for the granting of a license;
(5) Violation of the specified days or hours of operation or alcohol sales as set out in this chapter;
(6) Selling or dispensing alcoholic beverages during a period of suspension;
(7) Where the licensee's business or premises does not meet the licensing requirements of this chapter;
(8) Littering, loitering or public consumption of alcohol in the area of the licensed business or premises;
(9) Any other factor known to or discovered by the governing authority whereby it is shown that the licensee, any employee of licensee or any person holding any interest in a license, has engaged in conduct at or involving the licensed business or premises, or has permitted conduct on the licensed premises, that constitutes a violation of federal or state law, local ordinance or administrative regulations involving alcoholic beverages, gambling or narcotics. With respect to this subsection, it may be inferred that the violation was done with the knowledge or consent of the licensee; provided that such inference may be rebutted; and
(10) A licensed premises fails to open for operation or business within six months after the granting of the license.
(c) The governing authority shall provide a written statement of ground(s) to the applicant or licensee, as the case may be, and the governing authority shall provide such statement by personal service or by certified mail, return receipt requested, to the applicant or licensee, or the registered agent of the licensee. Within ten days of the giving of such statement, the applicant or licensee shall have the right to request a hearing on the statement, and the applicant or licensee shall do so by filing a written hearing request with the city clerk. Upon receipt by the city clerk of the hearing request, a hearing shall be scheduled within 20 days, and the applicant or licensee, or the registered agent of the licensee, shall be notified in writing, at least five days before such hearing, of the date, time, and place of the hearing (the governing authority's notice of hearing of hearing shall be served by personal service or certified mail, return receipt requested, to the applicant or license, of the registered agent of the licensee). The hearing shall be before the governing authority, and at the hearing the applicant or licensee shall have the right to appear in person and by attorney and to have witnesses appear on the applicant's or licensee's behalf and to cross examine opposing witnesses and present evidence. The witnesses shall testify under oath, but rules of evidence shall not apply.
(d) In the event that the governing authority determines that there exists any ground for the denial of an application, the governing authority shall deny such application.
(e) In the event that the governing authority determines that there exists any ground for the suspension of a license, a period of license suspension shall be imposed by the governing authority for a term not to exceed 30 days.
(f) In the event that the governing authority determines that there exists any ground for the revocation of a license, the revocation of a license by the governing authority shall be for the remaining calendar year of the year in which the revocation is imposed.
(g) The governing authority's decision regarding the denial of an application or the suspension or revocation of a license shall be personally served or mailed by certified mail, return receipt requested, to the applicant or licensee, or the registered agent of the licensee, within five business days of the close of the hearing. The decision of the governing authority shall constitute final action by the governing authority, subject to review upon petition for certiorari to the Superior Court of Toombs County. Georgia, unless another manner of appeal is required by Georgia law.
(h) Notwithstanding anything in this chapter to the contrary, upon the licensee's receipt of any statement of grounds, the licensee may waive the right to a hearing and consent to any sanction permitted herein, or as otherwise may be permitted by the Code. Any consent entered under this chapter shall be in the form of a written decree, executed by the licensee and the governing authority, and such decree shall be considered final action and non-appealable.
(i) Notwithstanding anything in this chapter to the contrary, upon the revocation of a licensee's state alcohol or alcoholic beverage license, the licensee's license issued under this chapter shall be immediately revoked by operation of law. The chief of police of the city or his sworn officers, upon notice of such revocation, shall take the necessary steps to ensure the immediate cessation of the licensee's sale of alcoholic beverages.
(j) Notwithstanding anything in this chapter to the contrary, the governing authority may exercise any other remedy at law or equity to enjoin a license or licensee, temporarily or permanently, in connection with any exigency that arises involving the licensed premises or location, where the governing authority finds that action is required for the protection of the public health, safety, or welfare of citizens of the city.
(Ord. No. 1698, § I, 5-3-2022)

160:
No licensee shall do any of the following upon the licensed business or premises:
(1) sell or distribute any alcoholic beverage to a person under 21 years of age;
(2) sell or distribute any alcoholic beverage to any person while such person is in an intoxicated condition;
(3) sell or distribute any alcoholic beverage on any day or at any time when such sale is prohibited by law; or
(4) allow any person to consume any alcoholic beverage on the premises of a person not licensed for the retail sale of any alcoholic beverage by drink for consumption on the premises only.
(Ord. No. 1698, § I, 5-3-2022)

168:
(a) A licensed retail dealer of alcoholic beverages by the package shall not sell, give away or otherwise distribute any alcoholic beverage during the following time periods:
(1) At any time when the sale is prohibited by state law;
(2) Monday: from 12:01 a.m. until 6:00 a.m.;
(3) Tuesday through Saturday: from 2:00 a.m. until 6:00 a.m.; and
(4) Sunday: from 12:01 a.m. until 12:30 p.m. and from 11:30 p.m. through 12:00 midnight; provided that, the sale of alcoholic beverages on Sunday under this subsection shall also be subject to and governed by section 6-28(a).
(b) A licensed retail dealer of alcoholic beverages by the drink for consumption on the premises only shall not sell, give away or otherwise distribute any alcoholic beverage during the following time periods:
(1) At any time when the sale is prohibited by state law;
(2) Monday: from 12:01 a.m. until 10:00 a.m.;
(3) Tuesday through Saturday: from 2:00 a.m. until 10:00 a.m.; and
(4) Sunday: from 12:01 a.m. until 12:30 p.m.; provided that, the sale of alcoholic beverages on Sunday under this subsection shall also be subject to and governed by section 6-28(b).
(Ord. No. 1698, § I, 5-3-2022)

171:
(a) In conjunction with any license application, or in conjunction with the issuance of any license, or in conjunction with the revocation, suspension or cancellation of any license, or in conjunction with the payment or nonpayment of any excise tax levied or to be collected under this chapter, the governing authority, the city manager, any sworn law enforcement of the city, and/or any code officer of the city, shall have the right to subpoena any and all records, books and documents, in any format, and any associated software, of any licensee for the purpose of auditing the records of such licensee, securing compliance by such licensee with the provisions of this chapter, proving or disproving any ground or any violation of any part of this chapter by any licensee, or showing payment or nonpayment of any taxes, fees or charges as contemplated by this chapter.
